1
00:00:00,067 --> 00:00:04,779
Every fire sprinkler has a colored glass bulb, and every color is a different

2
00:00:04,819 --> 00:00:05,520
temperature.

3
00:00:06,433 --> 00:00:06,936
Orange.

4
00:00:06,976 --> 00:00:11,746
The lowest number in the whole system: 135 degrees Fahrenheit.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,347
That little glass tube is filled with liquid, with one small bubble left inside on

6
00:00:17,387 --> 00:00:17,872
purpose.

7
00:00:17,912 --> 00:00:23,194
As the air around it heats up, the liquid expands and squeezes the bubble smaller

8
00:00:23,234 --> 00:00:23,982
and smaller.

9
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:29,086
When the bubble has nowhere left to go, the pressure shatters the glass, the plug

10
00:00:29,126 --> 00:00:32,046
above it falls out, and water hits the deflector.

11
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:37,682
Orange heads are meant for ceilings that never get hotter than 100 degrees.

12
00:00:37,722 --> 00:00:43,217
The rating sits far enough above that, that a hot day can't reach it, but a fire

13
00:00:43,257 --> 00:00:43,494
can.

14
00:00:43,534 --> 00:00:45,155
The bubble isn't a flaw.

15
00:00:45,195 --> 00:00:46,123
It's the fuse.

16
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:47,322
Red.

17
00:00:47,362 --> 00:00:48,310
155 degrees.

18
00:00:48,350 --> 00:00:54,644
The same ordinary class as orange, and the head you're most likely to find in

19
00:00:54,684 --> 00:00:56,124
offices and homes.

20
00:00:56,367 --> 00:01:00,581
But heat isn't the only thing that can break the glass.

21
00:01:00,621 --> 00:01:06,616
On October 20, 2017, a high school cadet staying at a Holiday Inn in Dumfries,

22
00:01:06,656 --> 00:01:10,948
Virginia, hung his uniform on the sprinkler in his room.

23
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:12,267
The bulb broke.

24
00:01:12,307 --> 00:01:18,321
Water flooded 10 rooms, the kitchen and the dining area, and a lawsuit later asked

25
00:01:18,361 --> 00:01:19,798
for nearly $700,000.

26
00:01:19,838 --> 00:01:24,892
The glass can't tell the difference between a fire and a coat hanger.

27
00:01:26,033 --> 00:01:26,588
Yellow.

28
00:01:26,628 --> 00:01:31,092
175 degrees, and the start of the intermediate class.

29
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:35,668
Intermediate heads are for ceilings that get hot on their own.

30
00:01:35,708 --> 00:01:41,312
Under a skylight in direct sun, or in an attic below a bare roof, the air at the

31
00:01:41,352 --> 00:01:45,686
ceiling can climb well past 100 degrees with no fire anywhere.

32
00:01:45,933 --> 00:01:49,641
Yellow heads are rated for ceilings up to 150 degrees.

33
00:01:49,681 --> 00:01:54,290
In a room like that, an orange bulb wouldn't be waiting for a fire.

34
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:57,204
It would be waiting for a sunny afternoon.

35
00:01:58,367 --> 00:01:58,762
Green.

36
00:01:58,802 --> 00:01:59,631
200 degrees.

37
00:01:59,671 --> 00:02:05,576
The same class as yellow, built for the same kind of space, just with more room to

38
00:02:05,616 --> 00:02:06,011
spare.

39
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:08,679
That's the pattern behind the whole code.

40
00:02:08,719 --> 00:02:13,644
Each class is built for a hotter ceiling, and its bulbs break comfortably above it.

41
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:16,502
And 200 is lower than you might think.

42
00:02:16,542 --> 00:02:21,160
A green bulb breaks before a pot of water would even start to boil.

43
00:02:22,067 --> 00:02:22,436
Blue.

44
00:02:22,476 --> 00:02:23,417
286 degrees.

45
00:02:23,457 --> 00:02:27,015
This is the high class, and it's a big jump.

46
00:02:27,267 --> 00:02:32,849
Blue heads are built for ceilings that can sit at up to 225 degrees, like the space

47
00:02:32,889 --> 00:02:38,132
right around an industrial unit heater, where hot air blows straight up at the

48
00:02:38,172 --> 00:02:39,216
ceiling all day.

49
00:02:39,256 --> 00:02:43,009
In a space like that, a red bulb wouldn't be protection.

50
00:02:43,049 --> 00:02:45,516
It would be a leak waiting to happen.

51
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,111
If you're finding this useful so far, a like and a subscribe help the channel more

52
00:02:51,151 --> 00:02:52,304
than anything else.

53
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:53,747
Purple.

54
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:54,755
360 degrees.

55
00:02:54,795 --> 00:02:56,517
The extra high class.

56
00:02:56,557 --> 00:02:59,958
Some manufacturers call this color mauve.

57
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,894
And this is where the code gets confusing.

58
00:03:02,934 --> 00:03:06,344
Glass bulbs aren't the only thing that's color coded.

59
00:03:06,384 --> 00:03:11,161
On many sprinklers the metal frame is painted too, and the frame code is a

60
00:03:11,201 --> 00:03:12,853
completely different list.

61
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:18,765
In that list, a red frame means extra high, the same class as this purple bulb.

62
00:03:18,805 --> 00:03:20,643
A red bulb means ordinary.

63
00:03:20,683 --> 00:03:23,459
Same color, opposite ends of the scale.

64
00:03:24,367 --> 00:03:24,680
Black.

65
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:26,861
The only color that isn't one number.

66
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:34,563
Black covers two whole classes, very extra high and ultra high, everything from 400

67
00:03:34,603 --> 00:03:36,191
degrees up to 650.

68
00:03:36,231 --> 00:03:38,993
One common black rating is 500.

69
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:44,478
These go in spaces that run hot all day, near ovens, heaters and industrial

70
00:03:44,518 --> 00:03:48,212
processes, where anything less would open on its own.

71
00:03:48,252 --> 00:03:51,172
Black isn't a warning about the sprinkler.

72
00:03:51,212 --> 00:03:53,285
It's a warning about the room.

73
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,128
The thin bulb.

74
00:03:55,168 --> 00:04:00,173
Two red bulbs can have exactly the same rating and still not be the same.

75
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:04,694
Standard response heads use a bulb about 5 millimeters thick.

76
00:04:04,734 --> 00:04:08,602
Quick response heads use one about 3 millimeters thick.

77
00:04:08,867 --> 00:04:14,317
Less glass and less liquid means it heats up faster, so it breaks sooner, while the

78
00:04:14,357 --> 00:04:15,640
fire is still small.

79
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:21,131
That's why quick response heads go where saving lives is the main job, like offices

80
00:04:21,171 --> 00:04:21,925
and schools.

81
00:04:21,965 --> 00:04:24,240
The color tells you when it breaks.

82
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,350
The thickness tells you how fast it gets there.

83
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:29,454
No bulb at all.

84
00:04:29,494 --> 00:04:34,688
Some sprinklers hold the water back with a metal link instead: two small plates

85
00:04:34,728 --> 00:04:38,200
joined by solder that melts at the rated temperature.

86
00:04:38,433 --> 00:04:40,280
This is the older idea.

87
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:46,841
On August 11, 1874, a New Haven piano maker named Henry Parmelee patented what's

88
00:04:46,881 --> 00:04:51,434
considered the first practical automatic sprinkler head.

89
00:04:51,474 --> 00:04:57,503
He built it to protect his piano factory, and to lower his insurance bill.

90
00:04:57,767 --> 00:05:01,394
The first automatic sprinkler wasn't built to save lives.

91
00:05:01,434 --> 00:05:03,131
It was built to save money.

92
00:05:03,633 --> 00:05:08,086
And finally, the most important number on this list isn't a temperature.

93
00:05:08,126 --> 00:05:08,647
It's one.

94
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:13,067
In movies, one sprinkler goes off and the whole ceiling opens.

95
00:05:13,107 --> 00:05:14,696
That's not how it works.

96
00:05:14,736 --> 00:05:20,192
In a normal wet pipe system, every head is on its own, and only the ones that get

97
00:05:20,232 --> 00:05:21,345
hot enough break.

98
00:05:21,385 --> 00:05:23,177
Smoke doesn't set them off.

99
00:05:23,217 --> 00:05:23,856
Heat does.

100
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:32,594
On November 21, 1980, a fire started in a deli inside the MGM Grand in Las Vegas.

101
00:05:32,634 --> 00:05:34,174
85 people died.

102
00:05:34,433 --> 00:05:37,344
The resort had cost $106 million.

103
00:05:37,384 --> 00:05:44,141
Putting sprinklers in the places that didn't have them would have cost about

104
00:05:44,181 --> 00:05:44,945
$192,000.

105
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:49,958
One of the investigators later said that with sprinklers, it would have been a one-

106
00:05:49,998 --> 00:05:53,715
or two-sprinkler fire, and nobody would ever have heard about it.

107
00:05:53,967 --> 00:05:54,573
In U.S.

108
00:05:54,613 --> 00:06:01,774
building fires from 2015 to 2019 where sprinklers operated, just one sprinkler

109
00:06:01,814 --> 00:06:04,728
operated 77 percent of the time.

110
00:06:04,768 --> 00:06:09,252
Five or fewer were enough 97 percent of the time.

111
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:14,851
And where sprinklers were installed, the death rate per fire was close to 90 percent

112
00:06:14,891 --> 00:06:15,236
lower.

113
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:21,434
On February 20, 2003, 100 people died in The Station nightclub in West Warwick,

114
00:06:21,474 --> 00:06:22,417
Rhode Island.

115
00:06:22,457 --> 00:06:28,390
The building was old enough that it had never been required to have sprinklers.

116
00:06:28,430 --> 00:06:34,666
That same year, Rhode Island required them in every nightclub that holds 150 people

117
00:06:34,706 --> 00:06:35,271
or more.

118
00:06:35,533 --> 00:06:37,040
There is one exception.

119
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:42,418
In a deluge system, every head is open, and when it fires, they all discharge at

120
00:06:42,458 --> 00:06:42,754
once.

121
00:06:42,794 --> 00:06:45,309
Everywhere else, the movies are wrong.

122
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,106
That's what the investigator in Las Vegas meant.

123
00:06:49,146 --> 00:06:51,127
A one- or two-sprinkler fire.

124
00:06:51,167 --> 00:06:56,843
One small glass tube, one color, breaking at one number, right above the fire, and

125
00:06:56,883 --> 00:06:57,749
nowhere else.

126
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,951
What color are the sprinklers above you right now?

127
00:07:00,991 --> 00:07:03,165
Look up, and tell me in the comments.
